From: Craig Johnson <1545411+captainpacket@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:20:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] api: adopt a late wire into the KubeVirt bridge waiting for it

virt-launcher builds k6t-XXXX and enslaves the wire ONCE, at VM start, from
whatever it finds in the pod netns. meshnet realizes a veth only when BOTH
endpoints exist, which for a link between two KubeVirt VMs can be after the VM
has already started. The wire then lands correct, up, and orphaned: the bridge
holds only its tap, so the VM has no path off the box -- and nothing reports an
error. The link is present from every angle except traffic.

Measured 2026-08-29 on example-lab-shared: siteB-dis-sw01 and siteB-dis-sw02 each held an
UP, master-less pod980816f36b7 beside a k6t-980816f36b7 containing only
tap980816f36b7. Every vJunos-to-vJunos adjacency was 100% loss in both
directions with ARP never resolving, while vJunos-to-container links on the same
nodes were fine. Enslaving the two orphans by hand restored all of them to 0%
loss immediately, which is the proof this is the whole fault.

The analysis system collected all 59 devices green throughout, because collection runs over
management: a dead fabric certifies clean. The pings caught it, the snapshot
never would have.

koko 0013 (meshnet-cni) taught the VXLAN path to SEE a KubeVirt-renamed wire.
This is the same lesson for the veth path: the wire may be realized after the VM
owns the interface, so the component that creates it must also hand it to the
bridge.

Deliberately conservative: an absent bridge is the ordinary container case and
not an error; a link already on that bridge is left alone; and a link owned by a
DIFFERENT master is never stolen.

diff --git a/api/koko_api.go b/api/koko_api.go
index 5448046..77473d3 100644
--- a/api/koko_api.go
+++ b/api/koko_api.go
@@ -883,6 +883,9 @@ func configureLinkInNS(veth VEth) error {
 		if err := netlink.LinkSetUp(link); err != nil {
 			return err
 		}
+		if err := adoptIntoKubeVirtBridge(veth.LinkName, link); err != nil {
+			return err
+		}
 		for i := 0; i < len(veth.IPAddr); i++ {
 			addr := &netlink.Addr{IPNet: &veth.IPAddr[i], Label: ""}
 			if err := netlink.AddrAdd(link, addr); err != nil {
@@ -896,6 +899,66 @@ func configureLinkInNS(veth VEth) error {
 	})
 }
 
+// kubeVirtBridgeName mirrors virt-launcher's bridge-binding convention: it
+// fronts pod interface XXXX with a linux bridge k6t-XXXX and a tap, then
+// enslaves the pod interface (renamed XXXX-nic) to that bridge. The VM reaches
+// the wire only through the bridge.
+func kubeVirtBridgeName(linkName string) string {
+	return "k6t-" + linkName
+}
+
+// adoptIntoKubeVirtBridge enslaves a freshly realized wire end to the KubeVirt
+// bridge that is already waiting for it.
+//
+// WHY THIS EXISTS. virt-launcher builds k6t-XXXX and enslaves the wire ONCE, at
+// VM start, from whatever it finds in the pod netns. meshnet realizes a veth
+// when BOTH endpoints exist, which for a link between two KubeVirt VMs can be
+// after the VM has already started. The wire then lands in the netns correct,
+// up, and orphaned: the bridge holds only its tap, so the VM has no path off
+// the box and nothing reports an error -- the link looks present from every
+// angle except traffic.
+//
+// Measured 2026-08-29 on example-lab-shared: siteB-dis-sw01 and siteB-dis-sw02 each held
+// an UP, master-less pod980816f36b7 beside a k6t-980816f36b7 containing only
+// tap980816f36b7. Every vJunos-to-vJunos adjacency was 100% loss in both
+// directions with ARP never resolving, while vJunos-to-container links on the
+// same nodes were fine. The analysis system still collected all 59 devices green, because
+// collection runs over management -- a dead fabric certifies clean.
+//
+// Enslaving here is the same repair, done by the component that owns the wire.
+//
+// DELIBERATELY CONSERVATIVE: absent bridge is the ordinary container case and
+// is not an error; a link already enslaved to that bridge is left alone; and a
+// link owned by a DIFFERENT master is never stolen -- that is someone else's
+// topology decision, not ours to override.
+func adoptIntoKubeVirtBridge(linkName string, link netlink.Link) error {
+	br, err := netlink.LinkByName(kubeVirtBridgeName(linkName))
+	if err != nil {
+		// No KubeVirt bridge fronting this wire: a plain container pod.
+		return nil
+	}
+	bridge, ok := br.(*netlink.Bridge)
+	if !ok {
+		return nil
+	}
+	if link.Attrs().MasterIndex == bridge.Attrs().Index {
+		return nil
+	}
+	if link.Attrs().MasterIndex != 0 {
+		return nil
+	}
+	if err := netlink.LinkSetMaster(link, bridge); err != nil {
+		return fmt.Errorf("adopt %s into KubeVirt bridge %s: %w",
+			linkName, bridge.Attrs().Name, err)
+	}
+	// Enslaving can clear the operational state; make the wire usable.
+	if err := netlink.LinkSetUp(link); err != nil {
+		return fmt.Errorf("adopt %s into KubeVirt bridge %s: set up: %w",
+			linkName, bridge.Attrs().Name, err)
+	}
+	return nil
+}
+
 // hostVxLanConflicts finds vxlans already holding this VNI.
